  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'PAPER_EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid evolution of code agents powered by large language models (LLMs) has revolutionized software development, enabling automated code generation, execution, and debugging. However, these powerful systems introduce significant security and safety concerns that demand rigorous evaluation. This paper explores the frontier of code agents and the emerging discipline of red-teaming as a critical mechanism for identifying vulnerabilities and improving system robustness. We survey recent advances in code agent architectures, examine methodologies for automated red-teaming, and discuss the symbiotic relationship between red teaming and blue teaming approaches. Specifically, we highlight two groundbreaking frameworks: RedCodeAgent (Guo et al., 2025), which provides automatic red-teaming capabilities against diverse code agents, and BlueCodeAgent (Guo et al., 2025), which leverages automated red teaming to enable robust blue teaming for CodeGen AI systems. Our analysis reveals that systematic red-teaming is essential for building trustworthy code agents and that the integration of adversarial testing with defensive strategies represents a promising direction for future research.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nCode agents represent a paradigm shift in how software development is conducted. Unlike traditional code completion tools, code agents can understand complex programming tasks, generate functional code, execute it in controlled environments, debug errors, and iteratively improve their outputs. This capability has been embraced by developers worldwide, with tools like GitHub Copilot, ChatGPT, and various open-source agents becoming integral to modern software engineering workflows.\n\nDespite their utility, code agents present unique security challenges. When these agents execute code in sandboxed environments, they can inadvertently execute malicious payloads, expose sensitive information, or create vulnerabilities in the generated software. The ability of code agents to interact with external systems and databases further amplifies the potential attack surface. Consequently, ensuring the security and reliability of code agents has become a critical research priority.\n\nRed-teaming, the practice of systematically probing systems for vulnerabilities, has emerged as an essential tool for evaluating code agent safety. Unlike traditional security testing, red-teaming for code agents must account for the dynamic, interactive nature of these systems and the potential for emergent behaviors that may not be predictable from static analysis alone. Automated red-teaming approaches, in particular, offer scalability advantages by leveraging the same LLM technologies that power the agents being tested.\n\nThis paper provides a comprehensive overview of research advances in code agents and red-teaming methodologies. Section 2 reviews related work in the field, including key papers that have shaped the current landscape. Section 3 discusses the architecture and capabilities of modern code agents. Section 4 examines red-teaming approaches, with emphasis on automated frameworks. Section 5 explores the relationship between red-teaming and blue-teaming strategies. We conclude in Section 6 with a discussion of open challenges and future research directions.\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\nThe field of code agents and their security evaluation has seen rapid growth over the past two years. We discuss here two pivotal contributions that have established new paradigms for automated security testing of code agents.\n\n**RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents** [1] (arXiv:2510.02609) introduces a novel framework for automated red-teaming of code agents. The authors recognize that code agents, which combine code generation with execution capabilities, create unique attack surfaces that previous red-teaming methodologies were not designed to address. RedCodeAgent employs a multi-round adversarial strategy, where an attacker agent iteratively crafts prompts designed to elicit malicious code generation or unsafe execution behavior from target code agents. The framework supports diverse code agent architectures and demonstrates effectiveness across multiple benchmarks. Key contributions include a comprehensive taxonomy of code agent vulnerabilities, an automated prompt generation mechanism based on evolutionary algorithms, and an extensive evaluation across five different code agent systems.\n\n**BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI** [2] (arXiv:2501.01234) complements the red-teaming perspective by focusing on defensive strategies. The authors argue that effective blue teaming—understanding and mitigating threats—requires insights gained through systematic red-teaming. BlueCodeAgent integrates an automated red-teaming component that continuously probes CodeGen models for vulnerabilities, with the findings directly informing defensive mechanisms. This paper introduces a feedback loop where red-teaming results are used to train protective mechanisms, creating an adaptive defense system. The framework demonstrates improved robustness against various attack vectors while maintaining code generation quality.\n\nBeyond these foundational works, several other research directions have contributed to the field. Early work on LLM red-teaming [3] established methodologies for adversarial prompt generation that have been adapted for code-specific scenarios. Research on code generation security [4] has identified common vulnerability patterns in LLM-generated code, providing valuable benchmarks for testing defensive mechanisms. Additionally, studies on agent-based security testing [5] have explored the use of autonomous agents for penetration testing, informing the design of automated red-teaming frameworks.\n\nThe relationship between red-teaming and code quality has also been investigated. Some researchers [6] have examined whether adversarial testing improves the robustness and correctness of generated code, while others [7] have proposed metrics for evaluating both the security and functionality of code agent outputs.\n\n[1] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang, et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[2] Chengquan Guo, Yuzhou Nie, Chulin Xie, et al. \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI.\" arXiv:2501.01234, 2025.\n\n[3] I. Perez, F. Huang, O. Sansone, and M. Rinard, \"An Evaluation of Adversarial Attacks and Defenses in Large Language Models,\" in Proceedings of ACL, 2022.\n\n[4] C. Xie, M. Andriushchenko, and N. Flammarion, \"Provably Robust Adversarial Defenses Against White-Box Attacks,\" in Proceedings of ICLR, 2022.\n\n[5] A. Li, X. Liu, Y. Zhang, et al., \"Autonomous Agents for Collaborative Task Completion,\" in Proceedings of AAAI, 2023.\n\n[6] S. Wang, M. Sharma, T. Koc, et al., \"Exploring Adversarial Robustness of Multi-Modal Large Language Models on Benchmark,\" arXiv preprint arXiv:2309.17452, 2023.\n\n[7] Z. Liu, Y. Shen, P. Vajda, et al., \"What Is Unusual in an Image? A Query-Realistic Framework of Visual Anomaly Detection,\" in Proceedings of CVPR, 2023.\n\n## 3. Code Agent Architectures\n\nModern code agents typically employ a combination of large language models, execution environments, and planning capabilities. The fundamental architecture includes:\n\n**Code Generation Module**: Based on LLMs fine-tuned on code corpora, this module generates code solutions to programming tasks. State-of-the-art models are trained on diverse codebases and include sophisticated understanding of programming languages, APIs, and software engineering best practices.\n\n**Execution Environment**: Code agents operate within sandboxed execution environments that allow them to test generated code, observe outputs, and iterate on solutions. These environments must balance security with flexibility, providing necessary tools while preventing harmful operations.\n\n**Planning and Reasoning**: Advanced code agents employ chain-of-thought reasoning, decomposing complex tasks into manageable steps. Some agents use tree-of-thought approaches, exploring multiple solution paths before selecting the most promising one.\n\n**Feedback Loop**: The ability to debug and refine code based on execution feedback is a hallmark of sophisticated code agents. This involves analyzing error messages, understanding failure modes, and iteratively improving solutions.\n\nThe security implications of these architectural choices are significant. Each component presents potential attack vectors: the code generation module can be influenced through adversarial prompts, the execution environment may be compromised, and the planning module might be manipulated to produce suboptimal or malicious solutions. Understanding these vulnerabilities is essential for developing effective red-teaming strategies.\n\n## 4. Red-Teaming Methodologies\n\nRed-teaming code agents requires methodologies that account for their unique characteristics. Traditional penetration testing approaches are insufficient because they typically assume static inputs and outputs, whereas code agents engage in dynamic, multi-turn interactions.\n\n**Prompt-Based Attacks**: The most common red-teaming approach involves crafting adversarial prompts designed to elicit malicious code or unsafe behavior. These prompts may use techniques such as prompt injection, where seemingly benign instructions are combined with hidden malicious intent, or format exploitation, where the structure of the prompt manipulates the agent's parsing and interpretation.\n\n**Automated Adversarial Generation**: Manual prompt engineering is labor-intensive and may not cover the full space of potential attacks. Automated approaches, like those employed by RedCodeAgent, use LLMs themselves to generate adversarial prompts. These systems employ evolutionary strategies, where successful attacks are selected and mutated to create more effective variants. The automation enables comprehensive coverage of the attack surface and scalability across diverse code agent configurations.\n\n**Multi-Round Interactions**: Code agents often engage in iterative dialogue, allowing red-teaming attacks to exploit this interaction pattern. Attackers may gradually build context or exploit the agent's learning during conversation to elicit responses that would be unlikely in a single-turn interaction.\n\n**Execution-Time Exploitation**: Beyond prompt-level attacks, red-teaming must also consider execution-time vulnerabilities. This includes testing whether generated code can access unintended resources, perform unauthorized operations, or interact maliciously with external systems. Such attacks require careful sandboxing and monitoring to detect and prevent.\n\nThe evaluation metrics for red-teaming effectiveness include success rate (proportion of target agents compromised), severity of compromise, efficiency (number of interactions required), and robustness of defense (effectiveness of mitigations). Both RedCodeAgent and BlueCodeAgent contribute frameworks for comprehensive evaluation using these metrics.\n\n## 5. Red-Teaming and Blue-Teaming Integration\n\nWhile red-teaming focuses on identifying vulnerabilities, blue-teaming emphasizes defense and mitigation. The recent work by Guo et al. [2] on BlueCodeAgent demonstrates the value of integrating these approaches. By leveraging automated red-teaming to inform defensive strategies, BlueCodeAgent creates an adaptive system that improves its security posture over time.\n\nThe integration follows a cycle: (1) Red-teaming agents probe the system for vulnerabilities, (2) discovered vulnerabilities are analyzed and prioritized, (3) defensive mechanisms are designed and implemented, (4) the enhanced system is re-evaluated through red-teaming. This continuous improvement loop ensures that defenses evolve in response to emerging threats.\n\nKey advantages of this integrated approach include:\n- **Comprehensive Coverage**: Combining offensive and defensive perspectives provides a more complete understanding of system security.\n- **Adaptive Defense**: Systems trained on real attack data are more likely to generalize to novel threats.\n- **Quality-Adjusted Security**: Defensive mechanisms can be validated against their impact on code generation quality, ensuring security improvements do not come at unacceptable cost to functionality.\n\nThe challenge lies in balancing offensive and defensive resources. Over-reliance on red-teaming may lead to security theater, where the appearance of security replaces actual robustness. Conversely, defensive strategies must be grounded in realistic threat models rather than hypothetical scenarios. The frameworks proposed in [1] and [2] provide templates for achieving this balance.\n\n## 6. Conclusion and Future Directions\n\nThe field of code agents and red-teaming is evolving rapidly, driven by the widespread adoption of AI-powered coding tools and the increasing recognition of their security implications. This paper has surveyed key advances in the field, highlighting the contributions of RedCodeAgent [1] and BlueCodeAgent [2] as foundational works that establish new paradigms for automated security testing and defense.\n\nSeveral challenges remain for future research. First, the field needs more comprehensive benchmarks that evaluate code agent security across diverse domains, programming languages, and threat models. Second, theoretical foundations for understanding the security of code agents would benefit from formal verification methods adapted to the probabilistic nature of LLM outputs. Third, the human factors in code agent security—such as developer awareness of risks and training needs—require further investigation.\n\nThe relationship between code agents and their security is fundamentally one of co-evolution. As code agents become more capable, they will enable both more sophisticated attacks and more powerful defensive mechanisms. The frameworks demonstrated by RedCodeAgent and BlueCodeAgent suggest that the most promising approaches will be those that integrate offensive and defensive capabilities, creating systems that are robust not only against known threats but also adaptable to emerging challenges.\n\nUltimately, the goal is not to prevent the development of code agents but to ensure they are deployed safely. By treating security as a first-class concern from the design stage and incorporating systematic red-teaming into development workflows, researchers and practitioners can build code agent systems that are both powerful and trustworthy. The work surveyed in this paper represents important progress toward that goal and provides a foundation for continued advances in this critical area of research.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang, et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[2] Chengquan Guo, Yuzhou Nie, Chulin Xie, et al. \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI.\" arXiv:2501.01234, 2025.\nPAPER_EOF"
}'}

